.TH GXWI-SD-EDITOR 1 "2026-10-02" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-sd-editor \- edit a security descriptor in a dialog on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-sd-editor
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opened by another program, which wants the security descriptor of something
of its own edited: a file, a registry key, a service. That program starts it
as a child, on the desktop it is on, and the two speak over the editor's
standard input and output, one JSON object a line. The program's first line
says what the object is called, what kind of thing it is, what its rights are
called, and what its descriptor is. The editor shows it in a dialog of its
own. When the person presses Apply or OK, the editor writes the whole
descriptor back, with the parts of it that changed, and the program applies
it and answers whether it could. The editor changes nothing itself.
.PP
The dialog shows the owner, and everyone the access list names. For whoever
is picked it has a box for each general right the program named, allowed and
denied. Inherited entries are shown greyed, and anything the boxes cannot say
is shown as Special and kept as it is. Add puts a user or group in the list
by name, Remove takes away what was set here for whoever is picked, and
Change sets the owner, where the program can change it.
.PP
The editor ends when the dialog is closed or cancelled, and after OK once the
program has applied what it sent. It ends as well when its standard input
closes, and when the program that started it does.
.PP
Started by hand, with no request on its standard input, it says so and ends.
.SH SEE ALSO
The protocol is set out in the Peios userspace protocols (PSPU), on
learn.peios.org.
